{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgg4qDwJDNKiGBhixVJUg","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For MnCl₂ I get **Dq ≈ 622 cm⁻¹, B ≈ 768 cm⁻¹ and C ≈ 3138 cm⁻¹** (C/B ≈ 4.1, 10Dq ≈ 6220 cm⁻¹). I used the full Tanabe–Sugano quartet matrices for d⁵, not the weak-field shortcut formulas.\n\n**Setup.** High-spin Mn²⁺ has a ⁶A₁g ground state, and I set its energy to 0. All three bands are spin-forbidden transitions to quartet states. In the strong-field (t₂ᵐeⁿ) basis the two needed matrices are:\n\n⁴T₁: \n| −10Dq+10B+6C | 3√2B | C |\n| 3√2B | 19B+7C | 3√2B |\n| C | 3√2B | 10Dq+10B+6C |\n\n⁴T₂: \n| −10Dq+18B+6C | √6B | 4B+C |\n| √6B | 13B+5C | −√6B |\n| 4B+C | −√6B | 10Dq+18B+6C |\n\nAs a check, at Dq = 0 these give the free-ion terms: ⁴G = 10B+5C, ⁴P = 7B+7C, ⁴D = 17B+5C and ⁴F = 22B+7C.\n\n**Step 1: the ⁴E band.** The ⁴A₁g/⁴E(G) level does not depend on Dq:\n10B + 5C = 23 370, so C = 4674 − 2B.\n\n**Step 2: substitute C into the other two matrices.** Write x = 10Dq.\n\n- ⁴T₁ is the lowest root at 19 180 cm⁻¹. Setting the determinant to zero gives:\n  4190(183 277 444 + 13 538B − 56B²) = x²(13 538 + 5B)\n- ⁴T₂ is the lowest root at 22 130 cm⁻¹. Setting the determinant to zero gives:\n  (1240+4B)(10 588+8B)(1240+3B) − x²(1240+3B) − 12B²(10 588+8B) = 0\n\n**Step 3: solve numerically.**\n- Eliminating x² and narrowing in on B (trials at 650, 700, 760, 800 and 768) gives B ≈ 768 cm⁻¹.\n- Then x² ≈ 3.873×10⁷, so 10Dq ≈ 6224 cm⁻¹ and Dq ≈ 622 cm⁻¹.\n- C = 4674 − 2(768) ≈ 3138 cm⁻¹.\n\nThese values are physically reasonable. B is about 80% of the free-ion value (~960 cm⁻¹), which shows moderate nephelauxetic reduction. A small Dq is expected for chloride.\n\n{\"Dq\": 622, \"B\": 768, \"C\": 3138, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Solved full d5 Tanabe-Sugano quartet matrices using field-independent 4E(G)=10B+5C and the 4T1(G), 4T2(G) bands.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"sto